      "id": "N30V7-F05",
      "severity": "critical",
      "summary": "A seventh live-verified defect found by N70's real production unit run -- the first ever to surface downstream of a genuinely successful WebSearch-backed locator discovery (N20V7-F13): D06B_RETRIEVE_SOURCE_CANDIDATES's `locator = locators[0]` is the whole locator OBJECT ({request_id, url, title, publisher, locator_kind, rationale} per the M01 schema), but `fetch(locator, authorization_receipt=authorization)` (N30V7-F03's own fix, earlier this lineage) passes that whole object as `SourceRetriever.fetch`'s `locator` parameter, which requires a bare URL string. Live-verified: the model found a real SparkFun Electronics tutorial via WebSearch and returned a well-formed locator; D06B's retrieval crashed with 'requested_target ... is not of type string' the instant it tried to use it -- this exact call path had never been reached with a real, well-formed locator by any prior run in this whole lineage, so N30V7-F03's own incompleteness (passing the object instead of `locator['url']`) went undetected until now. Fixed within N30's write set (nodes/sources.py): extracts `locator_url = candidate_field(locator, 'url')`, requires it to be a non-empty string (schema_contract failure otherwise, matching every other require() guard in this function), and passes that string to fetch. Every existing test fixture using a bare URL string in place of a real locator object (a shape D06B's real code path never actually produced, only ever accepted by coincidence) was corrected to the real M01 locator object shape; one of them was N30V7-F03's own regression test. Full N30 suite: 62 passed/27 subtests (cli/run_curriculum), 16 passed (D03/capability/D06B), 249 passed (deterministic_nodes, full file), zero scan violations, live driver_capabilities.ready: true for both drivers."
    }
